{
  "about": "What an extension changes in igni, and where two extensions collide: cases igni (src/ember_code/extensions/impact.py) and igni server (app/services/identity/impact.py) both run, so the two implementations of one rule give the same answers. The copy in igni's tests/fixtures/ is the original; igni server's tests/fixtures/ holds the same file, and each repository's test fails when its sibling checkout's copy differs. A case's \"priority\" is the super admin's order of extensions, highest first; every collision goes to the highest, and an extension the order does not name ranks below every one it does, by id. A case's optional \"reserved\" is the claims igni keeps for itself (its own commands), passed to the resolver as such. A manifest's \"permissions.hooks\" are never claims: extensions hooking one event are asked in priority order. A case lists every collision with its winner and its losers (\"collisions\"), what each extension loses (\"off\"), and the edits that land nowhere because another extension's override has no section they name (\"dropped\").",
